# PROSPECTIVE summary scaffold, generated by scripts/new-provenance-summary.sh.
# TODO: replace the placeholder summaryText/decisionPoint below with the
# actual distilled reasoning behind this PR before committing — see
# docs/plans/TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY_GUIDELINES.md. Timestamps below are real
# (from `git log main..HEAD` on branch test/527-query-timeout-http-test); everything else that
# isn't marked TODO can be left as-is.

ghpull:543 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "sparql_endpoint: add HTTP-layer test coverage for the query-timeout 503 response" ;
    bl:number 543 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:527 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr543 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:527 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-13T22:23:27+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-17T22:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr543Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #527 identified that PR #423's Deadline-cancellation timeout mapping in sparql_endpoint/src/query.rs (query_execution_error_response, a string-content match on Deadline::check's exact error message to distinguish 503 from a generic 500) had no HTTP-layer test: TestServer's helpers all hardcoded max_query_timeout_secs to 10, so nothing exercised the real /sparql route with a short enough timeout to trip it. Followed the precedent in sparql_endpoint/tests/request_timeout.rs (#367) for the sibling request_timeout_secs config, adding a matching start_writable_with_query_timeout constructor. The main design decision was how to make a query deterministically slow against a 1-second budget (the field is whole seconds, so 1s is the shortest usable value) without flaking on faster or slower CI machines. sparql_parser/tests/query_timeout_tests.rs sidesteps this by using a 1ms timeout that is 'already elapsed' before evaluation starts; that trick doesn't work for a real 1s wall-clock deadline, so an actually-slow query was needed instead. Benchmarked a transitive-closure property-path query (ex:p+) over synthetic chains of increasing length directly against sparql_parser::execute_with_base (bypassing the HTTP layer for fast iteration), initially in the default debug profile on this session's memory-constrained (3.7GB) environment, landing on a 300,000-node chain (~5.7s with no timeout in debug). CI's first run of this PR (Tests (release) job) then failed: that same 300k chain completed in well under 1s under `cargo test --release`'s optimized build and returned 200 instead of 503 — release-mode optimization of the BFS loop was fast enough that a size picked against a debug-profile benchmark gave no real guarantee about the release profile CI actually exercises, especially on faster CI hardware. Re-benchmarked specifically against `cargo test --release` locally (not debug) using `/usr/bin/time -v` to also track peak RSS: 300k now took only ~2.3s, 500k ~3.9s (973MB peak), 700k ~2.5s (1.28GB peak), 1,000,000 ~3.6s (1.94GB peak, and reliably >1s through the real HTTP path end-to-end too), while 1.5M OOM-killed on this 3.7GB-RAM sandbox. Chose 3,000,000 nodes for the shipped test — well beyond what could be verified directly on this constrained machine, but justified by the roughly-linear ~1.9-3.7KB/node memory scaling observed up to 1M (projecting to ~6-11GB at 3M, comfortably within a standard 16GB GitHub Actions runner) and by the multi-second-per-1M-nodes timing trend giving a wide margin over 1s even for much faster CI hardware. Also tried a large Cartesian-product BGP (two unbound triple patterns joined with no shared variable) as an alternative slow-query shape, but even a few thousand rows per side caused the debug-mode benchmark process to be OOM-killed on this constrained machine, so it was rejected in favor of the chain/property-path shape, which stays memory-light (linear in chain length) while still being CPU-heavy enough to blow well past a 1s budget. Along the way, the shared CARGO_TARGET_DIR filled its 79GB disk (100% used, unrelated to this PR — accumulated build artifacts from many other worktrees) and had to be `cargo clean`-ed (reclaiming ~77GB) after confirming via `ps aux` that no other worktree had a build in flight, before quality-gate commands could run again. After pushing the 3,000,000-node version, CI's Tests (release) and Unit & Integration Tests jobs both passed, confirming the fix; documented the debug-vs-release sizing pitfall directly in the test file's doc comment so a future size adjustment doesn't repeat it." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:543 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr543 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr543Summary agp:decisionPoint session:pr543Decision .

session:pr543Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Chose a transitive-closure property-path query (ex:n0 ex:p+ ?x) over a 3,000,000-node linear chain as the deterministically-slow query for tripping a 1s HTTP-layer timeout, sized by benchmarking directly against `cargo test --release` (not debug) up to the largest size verifiable on a 3.7GB-RAM sandbox (1,000,000 nodes, ~3.6-6.6s), then extrapolating a further 3x safety margin for faster/higher-memory CI hardware, and confirmed green on CI's actual Tests (release) and Unit & Integration Tests jobs afterward." ;
    agp:alternative "First shipped a 300,000-node chain sized only from a debug-profile benchmark (~5.7s with no timeout). This passed locally but failed CI's Tests (release) job, because release-mode optimization made the same query complete in under 1s — a stark demonstration that a size validated only in one build profile carries no guarantee for another. Also considered a large Cartesian-product BGP instead of a chain/property-path query; rejected because even a few thousand rows per side OOM-killed the debug-mode benchmark process on this constrained machine, whereas the chain/property-path shape's memory stays linear in chain length." .
